/* book Worked example: Q: Two straights meet at I, chainage 2450.000 m, with a deflection angle of 40°. They are joined by a circular curve of radius 300 m, to be set out by Rankine's method with pegs at every 20 m of chainage. (a) Find the tangent length, curve length, long chord, apex distance and mid-ordinate. (b) Find the chainages of T₁ and T₂. (c) Tabulate the tangential angles and total deflections. (d) Find the length of transition for 80 km/h with c = 0.3 m/s³, and the shift.
   1. (a) Δ/2 = 20°. T = 300 tan 20° = 300 × 0.36397 = 109.19 m. l = π × 300 × 40 ÷ 180 = 209.44 m.
   2. Long chord = 2 × 300 × sin 20° = 600 × 0.34202 = 205.21 m. E = 300(sec 20° − 1) = 300 × 0.06418 = 19.25 m. M = 300(1 − cos 20°) = 300 × 0.06031 = 18.09 m.
   3. (b) Chainage of T₁ = 2450.000 − 109.19 = 2340.81 m. Chainage of T₂ = 2340.81 + 209.44 = 2550.25 m (not 2450.000 + 109.19).
   4. (c) First sub-chord to chainage 2360: c₁ = 2360 − 2340.81 = 19.19 m. Then nine full chords of 20 m reach 2540, and the last sub-chord is 2550.25 − 2540 = 10.25 m.
   5. Tangential angles δ = 1718.9 c ÷ R minutes: for 19.19 m, 1718.9 × 19.19 ÷ 300 = 109.96′ = 1°49′57″; for 20 m, 114.59′ = 1°54′35″; for 10.25 m, 58.72′ = 0°58′43″.
   6. Total deflections: peg 2360, 1°49′57″; 2380, 3°44′33″; 2400, 5°39′08″; 2420, 7°33′44″; 2440, 9°28′19″; 2460, 11°22′55″; 2480, 13°17′30″; 2500, 15°12′06″; 2520, 17°06′41″; 2540, 19°01′17″; T₂ at 2550.25, 20°00′00″.
   7. Check: the total deflection to T₂ equals Δ/2 = 20°00′00″.
   8. (d) V = 80 ÷ 3.6 = 22.22 m/s. Ls = 22.22³ ÷ (0.3 × 300) = 10 974 ÷ 90 = 121.9 m, rounded up to 125 m for setting out. Shift S = 125² ÷ (24 × 300) = 2.17 m.
   Answer: T = 109.19 m, l = 209.44 m, long chord 205.21 m, E = 19.25 m, M = 18.09 m. T₁ at 2340.81 m and T₂ at 2550.25 m; total deflections from 1°49′57″ at 2360 to 20°00′00″ at T₂. Transition 121.9 m (125 m adopted) with a shift of 2.17 m. */
