/* book Worked example 2: Q: A rising gradient of +2.0 % meets a falling gradient of −1.5 % at chainage 1500 m, RL 105.000 m. A summit curve 200 m long is used. Find the RLs on the curve at 50 m intervals and the highest point.
   1. Start of the curve at chainage 1500 − 100 = 1400 m, tangent RL 105.000 − 0.02 × 100 = 103.000 m. g₂ − g₁ = −0.015 − 0.020 = −0.035.
   2. Correction = −0.035 x² ÷ (2 × 200) = −0.0000875 x².
   3. x = 50: tangent 104.000, correction −0.219, curve RL 103.781 m. x = 100: tangent 105.000, correction −0.875, RL 104.125 m.
   4. x = 150: tangent 106.000, correction −1.969, RL 104.031 m. x = 200: tangent 107.000, correction −3.500, RL 103.500 m (check: 105.000 − 0.015 × 100 = 103.500 m).
   5. Highest point at x = −g₁ Lv ÷ (g₂ − g₁) = 0.020 × 200 ÷ 0.035 = 114.29 m, chainage 1514.29 m, RL 103.000 + 0.02 × 114.29 − 0.0000875 × 114.29² = 104.143 m.
   Answer: Curve RLs 103.000, 103.781, 104.125, 104.031 and 103.500 m at chainages 1400 to 1600 m; summit at chainage 1514.29 m, RL 104.143 m. */
